@meta
id: 24-13
title: Sort a block and drop the duplicates
chapter: 24
type: lesson
skills: ex-sort
par: 17
minutes: 4
vscode: an extension, or drag lines around by hand
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
The imports at the top of this provider, lines 1 to 8, have been added
to by three people and none of them looked at the ones already there.
Two are duplicated outright. The lint rule wants them sorted and
unique. Nothing below the blank line changes.

`sort` takes a range and sorts the lines in it. With `u` it also drops
lines that are exact duplicates of the one before them, which after a
sort means every duplicate. Sorting is byte order by default, so every
capital letter comes before every lowercase one; the lint rule happens
to agree.

@keys
| `:1,8sort u` | sort lines 1 to 8, keeping one of each |
| `:sort` | sort the whole file |
| `:sort i` | sort ignoring case |

@hints
Range first, then `sort`, then the flag.
--
`u` is unique. Without it the sort still works but both duplicates are
still there, next to each other.
--
`:1,8sort u` and Enter, `:wq`.

@solution
    :1,8sort u<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Fifteen keystrokes for what is, by hand, a genuinely annoying edit.

`:sort` is the reason the shift and join lessons were worth doing: it is
another verb with the same shape, and with the range it becomes precise.
`:sort` on its own sorts the whole file, which is almost never what you
want in code. `:1,8sort` sorts the import block and nothing else.
`:'<,'>sort` sorts the visual selection, and that is how most people
use it day to day: `V`, stretch it over the block, `:sort`.

`u` compares whole lines, so two imports of the same thing with
different spacing are not duplicates to it. Chapter 26 covers piping a
range through the shell's `sort` and `uniq` for the cases where vim's
own flags are not enough.

@start order.provider.ts
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../customer/customer.repository';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
    @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService,
  ) {}
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { CustomerRepository } from '../customer/customer.repository';
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
    @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService,
  ) {}
}
